    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The oldest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-arabia">South Arabian</a> kingdoms, centred on the irrigated oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yemen">Yemen</a>, and the biblical land of Sheba.</li><li>It grew rich on irrigated farming and on frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#myrrh">myrrh</a> carried by caravan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</li><li>Rivals, sea trade and the rise of Himyar wore it down until Himyar absorbed it around 275 CE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Besides formal inscriptions on stone, the Sabaeans wrote letters and contracts in a cursive script on palm-leaf stalks, and many of these everyday texts have been recovered.</p></aside>
    </section>

