    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-ii.jpg" alt="Sargon II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_II,_Iraq_Museum_in_Baghdad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Saba, the biblical Sheba, emerged in the early first millennium BCE on the eastern edge of the Yemeni highlands, where the wadis descending from the mountains meet the desert of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramlat-al-sabatayn">Ramlat al-Sab’atayn</a>. Its heartland was the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a>, watered by the seasonal floods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadi-dhana">Wadi Dhana</a>, and the nearby town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sirwah">Sirwah</a>. Inscriptions in the Sabaic language, written in the Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-arabia">South Arabian</a> alphabet, appear by around the eighth century BCE, although the chronology of the early rulers is debated and some scholars place the beginnings earlier. The early rulers bore the title <em>mukarrib</em>, often translated ‘federator’, which marked their leadership over a federation of tribes and communities united in the cult of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almaqah">Almaqah</a>. Assyrian records mention Sabaean rulers sending gifts: Itamra the Sabaean to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> in 715 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karibil-watar">Karibilu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> c. 685 BCE, names usually identified with the mukarribs Yitha’amar and Karib’il. The great expansion came under Karib’il Watar in the early seventh century BCE, whose long inscription at Sirwah records campaigns that destroyed the Kingdom of Awsan, subdued towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-jawf-yemen">Jawf</a> such as Nashshan and imposed Sabaean supremacy over much of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#marib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marib.jpg" alt="Marib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MaribCity001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">بدرالدين الشهاري, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saba rested on two foundations: irrigated agriculture and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#incense">incense</a> trade. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a>, earthen and stone works diverted the floods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadi-dhana">Wadi Dhana</a> into channels that watered two great oases, together covering several thousand hectares; these works were enlarged over many centuries into the famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib-dam">Marib Dam</a>, whose surviving sluice structures belong to later phases. The fields produced grain, dates and other crops for a substantial urban population. Saba also lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> that carried frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#myrrh">myrrh</a>, gathered from trees in Hadramawt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dhofar">Dhofar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, north through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a> to Gaza and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Incense was in heavy demand for temple ritual and funerals across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a> and later the Greek and Roman worlds, and the profits of the trade made the Sabaeans proverbially rich in the eyes of classical writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>. Society was organised in tribal communities, each called a <em>sha’b</em>, bound to the ruler, with leaders known as <em>qayl</em>. Religion focused on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almaqah">Almaqah</a>, worshipped at the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#awwam-temple">Awwam</a> temple near Marib, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baran-temple">Bar’an</a> temple and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sirwah">Sirwah</a>, alongside deities such as ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athtar">Athtar</a> and the sun goddess Shams; pilgrimages, ritual hunts and dedications in bronze and stone are well attested.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#strabo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strabo.jpg" alt="Strabo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strabo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thévet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sabaean power was built by military campaigns as well as by trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karibil-watar">Karib’il</a> Watar’s inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sirwah">Sirwah</a> lists campaigns against Awsan, Qataban, the towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-jawf-yemen">Jawf</a> and other neighbours, with claims of many thousands killed and captured and towns burned, although such figures are probably inflated in the manner of royal annals. Towns were protected by massive walls of dressed stone with towers, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a> and Sirwah, and the walled city of Marib was among the largest in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a>. The tribal communities supplied fighting men to the ruler, and later inscriptions describe armies drawn from Sabaean tribes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bedouin">Bedouin</a> allies, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camel">camels</a> giving mobility across the desert margins. Between the sixth and first centuries BCE Saba was often on the defensive as Qataban, Hadramawt and Ma’in grew stronger. In 25 BCE a Roman expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a>, prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia-felix">Arabia Felix</a> with Nabataean guides and besieged Marib, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a> calls Mariaba, for six days before withdrawing for lack of water. In the first three centuries CE Saba fought long wars with Himyar, Hadramawt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>, whose troops intervened in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yemen">Yemen</a>, and kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilsharah-yahdub">Ilsharah Yahdub</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shairum-awtar">Sha’irum Awtar</a> recorded their campaigns in dedications at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#awwam-temple">Awwam</a> temple.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sabaean government evolved over a thousand years. The early mukarribs combined political and religious leadership, presiding over a federation of communities bound by covenant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almaqah">Almaqah</a> and leading rituals such as the communal hunt. Later rulers used the title <em>malik</em>, ‘king’, and the mukarrib title fell out of use; the change is usually placed around the middle of the first millennium BCE and may reflect a more secular kingship, although its date and meaning are debated. Royal power worked through tribal and communal structures: the sha’b communities were led by qayls who owed military service and dues, and councils of notables appear in the inscriptions. Decrees on stone regulated land, irrigation and the obligations of communities, and temples held estates and received tithes. Sabaic served as the language of a large epigraphic culture: thousands of inscriptions on stone and bronze survive, and everyday letters and contracts were also written in a cursive script on palm-leaf stalks, many of which have been recovered; radiocarbon dating places the earliest in the early first millennium BCE. From the first century BCE power shifted from the old families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a> towards highland tribes of the Hamdan confederation, and rival lineages competed for the throne and for the title ‘King of Saba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dhu-raydan">Dhu Raydan</a>’.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hebrew-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible.jpg" alt="Hebrew Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saba was the first and longest-lived of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-arabia">South Arabian</a> kingdoms, and its language, script and religious forms set the pattern for Qataban, Hadramawt, Ma’in and Himyar. Sabaean influence also crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-sea">Red Sea</a>: in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE the Ethiopian highland kingdom of D’mt adopted Sabaean-style temples, inscriptions and gods, as the great temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yeha">Yeha</a> shows, and the Ge’ez script of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> later developed from the South Arabian alphabet. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#incense">incense</a> trade carried Sabaean goods and reputation across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Greek and Roman writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a>, quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strabo">Strabo</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> made the Sabaeans a byword for the fabulous wealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia-felix">Arabia Felix</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>’s story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a> visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#solomon">Solomon</a>, retold in the Qur’an, where she is unnamed but later tradition calls her Bilqis, and in the Ethiopian <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a></em>, which makes her the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#menelik-ii">Menelik</a> I, gave Saba a lasting place in world literature and art, although no Sabaean inscription mentions such a queen. The Qur’an also recalls the bursting of the dam, the <em>Sayl al-‘Arim</em>, as a punishment of Saba. In 2023 the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sirwah">Sirwah</a> and the dam were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#abraha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraha.jpg" alt="Abraha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#abraha">Abraha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balami_-_Tarikhnama_-_The_miraculous_rescue_of_the_Ka%27ba_from_Abraha%27s_attack_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Abu Ali Bal'ami, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saba’s long decline began in the last centuries BCE. The rise of Qataban and Hadramawt, and Ma’in’s control of much of the northern caravan trade, had already reduced Sabaean dominance, and from about the second century BCE Greek and then Roman ships, sailing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monsoon">monsoon</a> winds between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, carried a growing share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#incense">incense</a> and eastern goods by sea, bypassing the overland route past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a>. At the same time new powers rose in the highlands, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#himyarite-kingdom">Himyarites</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zafar-yemen">Zafar</a>, who formed a rival kingdom in the late second century BCE. For three centuries Saba and Himyar contested the title ‘King of Saba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dhu-raydan">Dhu Raydan</a>’, with highland tribal dynasties ruling at Marib and alliances shifting between Saba, Himyar, Hadramawt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>. In the early third century CE Sabaean kings were still able to fight back: according to his inscriptions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shairum-awtar">Sha’irum Awtar</a> defeated Hadramawt and took its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shabwa">Shabwa</a>. Around 275 CE, however, the Himyarite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shammar-yahrish">Shammar Yuhar’ish</a> brought Saba under his rule and later added Hadramawt, unifying south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a>. Marib remained an important town, and its dam was repaired by Himyarite kings and in the sixth century by the Aksumite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abraha">Abraha</a>, but it finally broke, probably in the later sixth century, and the oasis was largely abandoned.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Saba was the oldest and most enduring of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-arabia">South Arabian</a> kingdoms, whose irrigation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marib">Marib</a> and control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#incense">incense</a> routes created a literate, wealthy civilisation for a thousand years, spread its script and gods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and lives on in the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>.</em></p>

